    "created_at": "2019-09-30T15:39:31Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-09-30T15:39:31Z",
    "body": "> What does ISO do here?\r\n\r\nI think my version of the ISO standard is not completely up-to-date, but it has a section “Representations with decimal fraction” that gives a *very* open interpretation. Basically saying they can be included “[i]f necessary”, that they can exist on all parts of time (hours, minutes, seconds) as long as you do not include any lower order parts, and that “interchange parties, dependent upon the application, shall agree the number of digits in the decimal fraction”. So honestly it is completely up to the HTML spec to codify what the interchange parties (UAs and authors) should stick to. It does state a preference for comma (`,`) as a decimal separator. ([Also cited on Wikipedia.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_8601#cite_note-25)) \r\n\r\nTo me it feels like HTML is only very loosely following ISO here currently. Not following their preferred decimal to express fractions, nor supporting fractions on anything other than seconds. I am unsure whether what ISO does should have any impact on the question.\r\n\r\nThe more specific answer would be to simply quote the format for decimal fraction representations straight from my version of the spec (original used underlines to signify repeating numbers, I am using `<b>` for GitHub Markdown support):\r\n\r\n> The format shall be [hhmmss,s<b>s</b>], [hhmm,m<b>m</b>] or [hh,h<b>h</b>] as appropriate (hour minute second, hour  minute,  and  hour,respectively),  with  as  many  digits  as  necessary  following  the  decimal  sign.  A decimal  fraction  shall  have  at  least  one  digit. \r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> UA parsing requirements and authoring conformance requirements are in general separate, and support in parsing does not imply something should be valid for authoring.\r\n\r\nI understand that, I just found it a little jarring in this case. There are lots of specific cases where date/time parsing errors out, but not here. And while I know the specification does not document how to create strings from the parsed time concepts again, the fact that if you were to do so in a trivial way leads to non-valid time strings sits wrong with me too.\r\n\r\nNot to mention that the choice of 3 decimals seems utterly arbitrary.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
